# Ordinary import integrity: bounded optimization proposal

Status: STATIC_DESIGN_ONLY. No scoped validator, session cache, schema change,
source execution, or throughput qualification is included in this proposal.

## Measured baseline and completed narrow changes

Exact source fb39ed3cb1e23ffa3be8bd768c1dd64216f110da imported 1,000 retained
TXT books / 20,000 segments through ordinary LibraryApp.add_text. Actual import
time was 969.628493533 seconds; min/max were 0.031841454 / 1.928042567 seconds.
The complete three-process run was 1,155.645278569 seconds. These are one
synthetic Linux corpus/configuration, not a general 10k/50k or native claim.
Exact artifact/phase evidence: blob8d0c4bbaad3b39f411fce2fde644618509d11056.

Two independent source slices are now reviewable, not executed:
- f436ba4fff6d879d381aa79a39a0404131ebcb89: parameterized revision equality
  replaces a nullable-OR locator predicate. All full audits stay.
- 0de49b4c2e8679b1928e8b4e38d3a67c3542005e: explicit checked-commit operation
  removes only a redundant full audit after the same catalog publication.
  Actual attempt receipt staging is inside the final rejectable full audit.

Neither change makes the remaining per-publication global audit constant-size.

## Existing boundaries that must remain explicit

Storage.stage_revision already validates the exact schema, original Page Python
values, per-page digest, ordinal/locator sequence, nonempty revision, seal,
active pointer and transactional old-index deletion/new-index insertion.
Canonical indexed_ai/indexed_ad triggers update FTS5 in that same transaction.
Catalog registration rejects work/edition/manifest conflicts and validates the
requested collections. The assembled publisher adds source_metadata and
source_page_review bindings. The worker binds its immutable per-attempt
import_publication row to exact request, catalog, source metadata and page bytes.
The journal can be completed only after publication has committed.

LibraryApp cold/open calls Storage.check. Storage.check/backup/rebuild and
migration validation run the full database/schema/foreign-key/seal/active/
locator/raw-digest/index/FTS/research/academic audit. Existing historical reads
rehash their selected page; retained-source search independently hashes its
selected original. Replay/recovery compare exact checkpoint/source/identity and
retain an independent full audit. None of these checks is a cached claim.

Current _CatalogPublisher also full-audits unrelated old content on every fresh
publication. That is actual existing behavior. The inspected negative suites
exercise selected candidates, replay membership, full cold/backup audits and
selected historical reads; no inspected case explicitly makes unrelated old
raw corruption the reason a different new publication must fail. This bounded
test observation is not a reason to remove a current behavior silently.

## Proposed scoped publication contract

A fresh-publication receipt would mean only:

"The selected original and extraction configuration, requested catalog metadata,
all newly published source pages and locators, selected prior-active transition,
requested review records and this attempt's receipt committed together after
validation."

It would not certify unrelated retained revisions, saved research, bibliography
or arbitrary prior search content. Those remain covered by full cold/open,
manual, backup, migration, replay/recovery and relevant live-source read checks.
No serialized 'library verified' flag, data_version counter, file mtime, or
cross-connection cache may be used as authority.

A scoped operation would use the existing exclusive_writer and one existing
BEGIN IMMEDIATE publication transaction. It would use bound equality queries,
with no new persistent schema or bypass of the native worker.

### Exact validation plan inside that transaction

1. Recheck the exact released schema and required native foreign-key/recursive
   trigger settings before mutation and again before commit. Do not accept a
   same-name replacement trigger, temporary writable-schema window, missing
   trigger, attached catalog, or ended transaction.
2. Capture the selected prior active revision and its source/index identity.
   Validate the affected prior source rows before using their raw text to
   remove FTS postings. The previous revision/seal stays immutable and readable.
3. Freeze the explicit registration values and review records. Accumulate a
   count and canonical ordered digest from the original Page values supplied to
   stage_revision, without copying every raw string a second time. This is an
   ephemeral input commitment, not a catalog trust cache.
4. After staging, stream only the selected revision in ordinal order. Compare
   the complete identity/config, original values, page count, every raw digest
   and ordered commitment; invoke _check_locators(revision). Require its seal,
   exact active pointer and exact corresponding indexed rows. Refuse missing,
   extra, wrong-raw or out-of-scope rows for that transition.
5. Require every old active page to have left indexed, without deleting its
   historical revision/page/source bytes. Do not infer this only from row
   counts, old/new token overlap, or successful search.
6. Re-read the exact selected work/authors/edition/asset/source_metadata and
   required collection memberships; compare to the frozen registration.
   Preserve workspace and active-collection authorization. Validate all selected
   source_page_review fields and their page digests.
7. Stage the current immutable attempt receipt through the existing producer.
   Its actual request/catalog/source/page binding remains authoritative. Any
   source/review/receipt/schema/FTS validation failure must still roll back the
   entire candidate and leave prior committed books and journal checkpoints.
8. Commit before returning completion. Later cancellation or observer/cleanup
   errors cannot turn that committed receipt into cancellation or authorize a
   retry. A failed transaction cannot reuse any successful-validation marker.

### FTS and native integrity are the unresolved asymptotic boundary

SQLite's documented FTS5 integrity-check with rank=1 verifies the complete
external-content index. It has no documented per-document/row-range form.
A rowid SELECT from an external-content FTS table reads the external content
and does not independently prove postings. Testing selected MATCH queries also
cannot establish absence of extra/corrupt postings or complete token/position
equivalence. A replacement must not label those checks as a full FTS audit.

A first scoped-semantic implementation could retain the current native
integrity_check, foreign_key_check and FTS5 integrity-check while narrowing only
Python raw/locator/research/academic work to the affected publication. That is
defensible as a reduction in repeated work, but still performs global native
work and therefore is not an asymptotic solution. It should not be presented as
a completed 10k/50k ordinary-import requirement.

A true delta-only FTS proof would need either a proven native API capable of
checking the affected postings, or a tightly owned finite transaction/session
whose permitted mutation protocol itself maintains that invariant. Neither is
provided by a data_version/total_changes cache alone. The current Python/SQLite
API should not be supplemented with an invented weak index proof.

## Session alternative and why it is not a drop-in patch

An explicit finite catalog import session could full-audit entry, own one
connection, validate known publication deltas, and invalidate on external/DDL/
unexpected same-connection changes. However, current paths complicate this:
- LibraryApp.import_files promises separately durable per-file results and
  invokes progress outside every import transaction. Existing real observers
  reopen LibraryApp. Holding the current writer lock across these callbacks
  changes a tested contract and can deadlock/reject those observers.
- ImportCenter runs one existing owned child per durable request, which creates
  a fresh LibraryApp. It currently performs a cold audit per file. A parent
  cache does not transfer trust to a new SQLite connection or child.
- A persistent connection changes Windows file-lifetime behavior. Existing
  workflows can delete or replace a closed old root while its LibraryApp object
  still exists. An app-lifetime connection must not silently break that.
- data_version detects other commits only relative to the same connection;
  total_changes is not a DDL/rollback/global-mutation authority. Counting expected
  DML does not detect all unexpected writes or a restored schema definition.
- Callbacks/generators and receipt producers can execute code between stages.
  If a session relies on only known writes, it must actually enforce that
  admission boundary rather than trust a final total_changes value.

A supported public session must therefore be an explicit finite user path,
with bounded lifetime, cooperative cancellation, independent observer points,
an enforced mutation protocol and honest per-item commit/recovery. The benchmark
must use that same supported path. A benchmark-only bulk seed or SQL bypass is
not ordinary-import throughput evidence.

## Required negative and real-boundary validation for any further change

Preserve existing atomic candidate/DOCX replay/terminal replay/schema/locator/
printed-label/FTS/worker receipt/retained-source/backup suites and their original
assertions. Add at least:
- exact candidate raw/digest/locator/source metadata/review/receipt tampering
  before commit, with a separate reader observing no partial candidate;
- missing/extra/wrong-raw new index entries and retained old-active postings;
- schema drop/restore, same-connection unexpected mutation, rollback followed by
  retry, and actual second-connection mutation between session transactions;
- external root/database replacement revoking a live session, with Windows
  connection ownership tested rather than inferred;
- unrelated historical corruption still refused by its relevant read,
  cold/open/backup/manual/recovery audits; no scoped result claims full health;
- actual cancellation before publication and late cancellation after immutable
  receipt commit, retaining exact source/checkpoint/result bytes;
- VM-operation or actual query-plan evidence that the *scoped* predicates do not
  traverse unrelated rows, separately from whole end-to-end throughput;
- actual supported-path 1k/10k/50k runs with the same capacity/resource limits.
  Do not replace the ongoing unmodified baseline jobs.

